/T (2. Repeated evaluation: MAX\(i++, j\) may evaluate i++ once in the comparison and again in the selected expression. This can change i more than once and make the result surprising. Avoid side-effect expressions as macro arguments. 3. gcc -E: Save the program as task4.c and run gcc -E task4.c. The preprocessed output shows the macro calls replaced by expressions such as \(\(\(a + 1\) * \(a + 1\)\)\). Header contents and line markers may also appear.)
/E (2. Repeated evaluation: MAX\(i++, j\) may evaluate i++ once in the comparison and again in the selected expression. This can change i more than once and make the result surprising. Avoid side-effect expressions as macro arguments. 3. gcc -E: Save the program as task4.c and run gcc -E task4.c. The preprocessed output shows the macro calls replaced by expressions such as \(\(\(a + 1\) * \(a + 1\)\)\). Header contents and line markers may also appear.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [31]
>>
