1849 0 obj
<<
/Title (The bill calculation for one household remains O\(1\) because it uses a fixed number of arithmetic operations. Processing n household bills takes O\(n\) time. A basic program can handle 800,000 bills if memory, input/output, and integer ranges are managed correctly. A production system should stream records or process batches, validate each record, log rejected records, and use checked wide-integer aggregation.)
/Dest [33 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1780 0 R
>>
endobj
1850 0 obj
<<
/Title (1. Read each interval record: meter ID, timestamp, and watt-hours. 2. Validate the meter ID, timestamp, and non-negative energy value. 3. Convert watt-hours to billing units \(kWh\) using 1 kWh = 1,000 Wh, retaining integer Wh internally where possible. 4. Identify whether the interval falls between 18:00 and 22:00 local time. 5. Apply the 20% peak multiplier to the energy rate for those intervals. 6. Aggregate energy charges in a sufficiently wide integer representation, with a documented rounding rule. 7. Add the fixed charge once per billing period, not once per interval. 8. Produce a bill summary and an audit trail.)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1783 0 R
>>
endobj
